WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.400
If you left the theater feeling a little disappointed
by the Lion King remake, one fan artist has

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.400
just the remedy for your blues.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:12.540
Digital artist and Photoshop wizard Nikolay
Mochkin, who goes by the username ellejart

00:00:12.549 --> 00:00:18.090
on Instagram, has reimagined the Lion King
characters as they're seen in the new film

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.800
and drawn them in a style more closely aligned
with the original movie.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.180
Originally voiced by Jonathan Taylor Thomas,
young Simba is played by JD McCrary in director

00:00:26.189 --> 00:00:27.480
Jon Favreau's remake.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.640
And he doesn't just sound different, either.

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:34.920
Gone are the exaggerated eye movements, head
tilts, winces, and smiles that Simba expressed

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:39.949
in the '90s movie, replaced instead by a lion
who looks real enough to reach out and touch...

00:00:39.949 --> 00:00:42.120
but isn't really as emotive as before.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:46.940
Mochkin brought back Simba's signature sass
in his re-working of the character's design,

00:00:46.949 --> 00:00:50.820
adding in downturned brows when the young
lion cub knows he's in trouble, and giving

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:55.000
him wider eyes as he looks out at the vast
expanse of the Pridelands.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:59.640
Adult Simba also got a retouch from Mochkin,
who gave the remake's version of the lion

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.660
his orange-tinged mane, bright eyes, and expressive
face from the original film.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:08.520
And though she's voiced by multi-Grammy-Award-winning
artist and chart-topping singer-songwriter

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:13.600
Beyoncé this time around, Simba's childhood
friend-turned-queen Nala still wasn't perfect

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.300
by Mochkin's estimations.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:20.140
He made her eyes brighter and bluer, her muzzle
squarer, and her ears larger and lower than

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:20.760
before.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:25.620
To some, the biggest mistake the Lion King
remake made was dampening the menacing presence

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.680
of Simba's villainous uncle Scar.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:32.920
Voice actor Chiwetel Ejiofor, known for his
work in films like 12 Years a Slave and Doctor

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:37.790
Strange, did a pretty bang-up job making Scar
sound as vicious as Jeremy Irons did in the

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:42.700
original Lion King, but most people felt that
Disney's animators failed to depict the mangy

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.040
lion in all his sinister wickedness.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:48.080
"Long live the King."

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:50.700
Mochkin, however, aimed to fix that.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:55.960
He reimagined Scar with his trademark black
mane, dark sienna coat, yellow eyes, and a

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.400
scar that runs down his forehead and cheek.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:03.250
On top of all that, Mochkin even reworked
the poster for The Lion King, in which Simba

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:08.100
and his ragtag guardians Timon and Pumbaa
look far more expressive than they did before.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:13.840
Though it was marketed as much, Disney's "live-action"
remake of The Lion King wasn't actually live-action

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:14.640
at all.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:19.580
It certainly was a remake, though, essentially
taking the exact shots used in the animated

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:23.820
original and reimagining them in photorealistic
CGI animation.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:28.610
The end result was visually stunning, but
it was also lacking a certain something which

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:33.580
many critics felt was the nuanced emotion
present in the 1994 version of The Lion King.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.300
"Talk about your fixer-upper."

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:40.460
The edits that Mochkin created are sure to
appease the fans who might have found themselves

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:45.480
disappointed by the Lion King's remake, but
they may also do something better still: motivate

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:50.860
Disney to hold off on just pumping out live-action
remakes of its classic animated films, and

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.980
take a moment to actually consider what the
best approach could be.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:58.420
There's no doubt that the nostalgia factor
behind The Lion King pulled audiences to theaters

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:04.830
in their droves, and it paid off, too: the
new Lion King made over $595 million worldwide

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:06.780
as of July 25th 2019.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.780
But box office bucks can only take a studio
so far, and if viewers aren't joyously picking

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:15.940
up what it's putting down, it might just be
time for Disney to figure out a plan to keep

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:20.200
its future live-action remakes from getting
hit with the same rotten reviews that the

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:20.860
Lion King did.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.100
And if that never happens?

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:24.640
Well, at least we got some cool artwork out
of it.

